{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If a man takes a vow to HaShem.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says 'a vow to HaShem' — not because a vow or an oath requires mention of the Name, for one who says 'I vow,' 'I swear that I will do such,' or 'that I will not do such' — this is a complete oath and he is liable for it even without mention of the Name; and therefore it did not say 'or takes an oath to HaShem.' And the reason it attached the Name to the vow and said 'a vow to HaShem' is to teach you that a vow takes effect even upon matters that belong to HaShem, such as one who vows to nullify a commandment — it is forbidden for him to perform it unless he regrets and they release it for him. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'נדר לה׳' (a vow to HaShem) teaches that vows take effect upon a matter of commandment just as upon a matter of permission. But regarding an oath it is not so: one who swears to nullify a commandment receives lashes on account of a vain oath and fulfills the commandment. And the tradition our Rabbis of blessed memory received, that the reason a vow takes effect upon a matter of commandment, is because a vow forbids the object upon the person — דנדר אסר חפצא אנפשיה — for he forbids upon himself the vowed thing, and that thing itself is what becomes forbidden, and that thing was not sworn about at Mount Sinai. But an oath forbids the person upon the object — אסר נפשיה אחפצא — for the one who swears forbids himself from doing a thing that he was already sworn to do, and himself was sworn about at Mount Sinai, and an oath does not take effect upon an oath. And regarding this distinction between a vow and an oath, we find the language of verses proving it: for we find regarding an oath (Leviticus 5:4): ״או נפש כי תשבע לבטא בשפתים להרע או להיטיב״ — to harm himself or to benefit himself, such as fasting today or eating and drinking today, which are matters of permission; and in the language of a vow we find: ״ככל היוצא מפיו יעשה״ — that is, whether it is a matter of commandment or a matter of permission." "<b>And the fact</b> that it says 'if a man takes a vow' — this means he must take the vow when he is a 'man,' thirteen years and above. If he vowed at twelve years and above, they examine him: if he knows for whose Name he vowed, his words stand, and likewise his consecrations are consecrated, and this is a halakhah given to Moses from Sinai. And so they expounded in tractate Arakhin: ״איש כי יפליא לנדור נדר נזיר״ — to include a 'mufleh' (one near majority) close to manhood. Now that it has been clarified that vows take effect upon a matter of commandment just as upon a matter of permission, therefore one who vows to fast on the Sabbath or on a Festival is obligated to fast — unlike one who swears. And likewise one who vows to fast every Monday or every Thursday all his days — that is, he forbade upon himself eating on those days, which is forbidding the object upon himself (מיסר חפצא עליה) — and a Festival or the eve of Yom Kippur falls on one of them, he is obligated to fast, unlike one who swears not to eat. But if Rosh Chodesh or Hanukkah or Purim falls on it, which are rabbinic and concerning which they decreed not to fast — some say he should set aside his vow and not fast on them, because the Sages made reinforcement for their words greater than those of the Torah. And this is what they said: an individual who accepted upon himself the fasts of Monday and Thursday and the Monday after [the festivals] throughout the year, and it happened that the days written in Megillat Ta'anit fell on them — if his vow preceded our decree, his vow nullifies our decree; and if our decree preceded his vow, his vow is set aside on account of our decree. And even though vows take effect upon a matter of commandment just as upon a matter of permission, and Festivals and Sabbaths require an 'opening' [for release of vows], this does not take effect because of the reinforcement that the Sages of blessed memory made for their words greater than those of the Torah — and so is the opinion of the Rambam of blessed memory." "<b>And there are</b> those who interpret 'he should set aside his vow' to mean that he goes to a sage who opens [a path of regret] for him through remorse, just as they open [for release] on Sabbaths and Festivals — and this is the correct view. But if he expressed a vow in the language of an oath, that he vows not to do such and such; or an oath that he made in the language of a vow, saying 'a vow is upon me that I shall not do such' — this is a dispute in the Jerusalem Talmud, and they are concerned for both the vow and the oath to be stringent. In what case? When one vows or swears from doing what he was commanded to do. But if he vowed or swore to do some thing that he refrained from doing, or to eat some forbidden thing — that is like one who swears to nullify a commandment deliberately, and the halakhah is: he receives lashes, he does not do it, and he does not eat it, even concerning rabbinic prohibitions, such as drinking untithed wine, or eating kil'ei ha-kerem outside the Land, or fowl with milk — for all these are from the words of the Sages of blessed memory. You can know this for yourself, for the shofar of Rosh HaShanah — one does not ascend a tree with it [to fulfill it elsewhere], for its ascent [the technicality] is a decree of the Sages of blessed memory, while the commandment of shofar — they were sworn about it at Mount Sinai. And so it says here: ״או השבע שבועה לאסור אסר על נפשו״ — our Rabbis of blessed memory said: to forbid the permitted, and not to permit the forbidden. One who swears or vows — whether on his own or through others and he answered Amen — even if a Gentile or a minor administered the oath to him and he answered Amen, he is liable, for anyone who answers Amen after an oath is like one who uttered an oath from his own mouth, for lashes and for an offering. And likewise anything whose purport is like Amen, such as saying 'yes,' or 'I am bound by this oath,' 'I accept this oath upon myself' — whether he swore by the special Name or by one of the epithets, by Him whose Name is Gracious, or Compassionate, or Slow to Anger, that he will not do such or will do such; or by 'alah' or by 'cursed be' — by Him whose Name is Gracious, whoever eats a certain thing and ate it, he has sworn falsely. And likewise the law of one who vows in all these [modes]. And one who intended an oath and resolved in his heart not to eat today and not to drink, and that this thing is forbidden by an oath upon him — this is permitted to him, since he did not express it with his lips, for it says (Leviticus 5:4): ״לבטא בשפתים״ — until he expresses the purport of an oath with his lips; and 'matters of the heart are not matters.' And likewise if he erred and expressed with his lips something he had not resolved in his heart, such as intending to swear not to eat at Reuben's and he said 'at Simeon's' — he is permitted to eat with both of them: with Reuben, because he did not express it with his lips, and with Simeon, because he did not intend it. And likewise if he intended to swear about barley bread and swore about wheat bread, he is permitted in both. And likewise the law of one who vows — until his mouth and his heart are equal. Therefore, one who swears in our presence not to eat today and ate, and they warned him, and he said: 'I only had it in my heart not to go out today, and my tongue erred and I expressed eating which was not in my heart' — this one does not receive lashes. But one who swears upon the understanding of others cannot say 'thus and so was in my heart,' for he did not swear on his own understanding but on theirs, and since their mouth and heart were equal, they stand in his place. And one who retracts his oath within the span of speech — which is the time it takes a student to say to his master, 'Peace be upon you, my master' — and he said 'this is not an oath' or 'I regret it' or 'I retract' or the like, whose purport is that he released what he had forbidden — the oath is uprooted, for this is analogous to 'questioning' [the immediate doubt that nullifies a sacrifice]. Thus wrote the Rambam of blessed memory in chapter 2 of Hilkhot Shevu'ot." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'If a man takes a vow to HaShem' — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Be careful with vows and do not breach them, for anyone who breaches them will end up violating oaths, and anyone who violates oaths has denied [Him], and there is no forgiveness for him in this world, as it says (Exodus 20:7): ״כי לא ינקה ה׳״, and another verse says (Jeremiah 4:2): ״ונשבעת חי ה׳ באמת במשפט ובצדקה״. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Israel: Do not suppose that it is permitted to swear by My Name in truth — you are not permitted to swear by My Name at all unless all these qualities are in you: (Deuteronomy 10:20) ״את ה׳ אלהיך תירא אותו תעבוד ובו תדבק ובשמו תשבע״. If all these qualities are in you, you are permitted to swear by My Name, and if not, you are not permitted to swear by My Name. Thus far." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> 'If a man takes a vow to HaShem' — נדר is from the language of dwelling (דירה); לה׳ is the dwelling of the world; Elohim, as in Genesis, who created the world — and the seven [oaths] emanate from that Name. One who fulfills his vow, it is known that he believes in that dwelling, when his soul returns there; and one who violates the vow — he removes himself from that dwelling. And this is the sense of נדר, which bears a thing and its opposite: the establishing of a dwelling and the removal of a dwelling. And likewise the language of נשבע: one who fulfills his oath enters within the seven, and one who violates it removes himself from them." "<b>And you should be roused</b> to ask concerning the language of Moses, who said זה הדבר אשר צוה ה׳ איש כי ידור נדר לה׳ — for this reason he needed to mention לה׳, and did not say כי ידור נדר אליו; and this is the power of the vow, that it is above the oath, for the oath is language derived from שבעה (seven), and therefore the vow is fitting to rest upon the commandments, for the commandments were given through seven — the seven voices at the giving of the Torah, as I have already explained there. And therefore the oath throughout the entire Torah comes with the letter bet, as in (Exodus 32:13) אשר נשבעת להם בך, (Psalms 89) אחת נשבעתי בקדשי, (Genesis 22:16) בי נשבעתי, (Deuteronomy 6:13) ובשמו תשבע, (Isaiah 62:8) נשבע ה׳ בימינו — for the right hand is part of the oath, and the word נשבע testifies to this; but the vow comes with the letter lamed: (Psalms 132:2) נדר לאביר יעקב, and so here, כי ידור נדר לה׳, (ibid. 24:1) לה׳ הארץ ומלואה — that the influx is cut off from the upper and lower beings and all returns to its first foundation. From here you will understand with clarity the statement of the masters of truth, who said that the oath is like swearing by the king himself, while the vow is like swearing by the life of the king, for the life of the king is the essence of the king — and this is clear." "<b>He shall not break his word.</b> He shall not profane — that he not make his words profane. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: it is he who may not profane, but others profane for him — that is, if he regrets having sworn and vowed from the outset and says, 'Had I known that such would be the case, and had I at that time the state of mind I have now, I would not have done it,' they permit it to him, for it is as though he erred. And no person is authorized to release his own oath or his vow, nor even his wife's vows in vows that the husband cannot annul in his wife, for his wife is like his own body. When are these words said? When there is a qualified individual present; but it may be that three laymen suffice, and this is a dispute among the commentators. And no person is authorized to release an oath or a vow in the presence of one greater than himself in wisdom, nor in the presence of his teacher, except with his teacher's knowledge." "<b>According to all that proceeds from his mouth he shall do.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted in tractate Nedarim: behold, it says לא יחל דברו, and what need is there to say ככל היוצא מפיו? Rather, from here we learn that euphemisms for vows are like vows, and euphemisms for oaths are like oaths. And this is its explanation: if the one swearing stammers with his lips, as in places of stutterers where one says for an oath שקוקה or שבוקה and the like — if their way is to call an oath מומתא and he says מותא and the like, this is an oath, and if he transgresses it, he has sworn falsely — for we do not follow the word itself but the sense of the matter, since everyone knows that their speech is thus. And likewise with euphemisms for vows, if in that place they say for a vow נזק or בזק — where they corrupt the word and remove it from its original form — we follow only the intent, and concerning this it is said ככל היוצא מפיו יעשה, for this is a complete oath and a complete vow, since he completed it in his heart and brought forth upon his lips what he knows. And even though he did not mention the Name at all, nor any of the epithets, he is liable for this oath, for a vow or an oath do not require mention of the Name; and if he transgresses it, he has sworn falsely. And all this stringently applies to the language of the oath, except that he does not receive lashes and does not bring an offering, since he did not mention the Name nor any of the epithets." "<b>Our Rabbis,</b> of blessed memory, said: the release of vows flies in the air and has nothing on which to rely — that is, they are words of received tradition and have no root in the written Torah. And thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received that a vow or an oath may have release by means of a sage or by means of three laymen, provided he regrets. And the Torah desired to remedy a person's stumble, for sometimes his anger overcomes him and he has no restraint at all, and he leaps and swears by the Name that he will do such-and-such or will not do such-and-such, and he further adds and multiplies his oath in his moment of anger and says it without any grounds for release or regret in the world; and after he calms and changes and becomes a different man, he will regret from the outset and his mind will change to a different mind, and with the regret he feels, he has release through a sage and returns to his original permitted state. And he must first regret what he swore, without which he obtains no release from this; and they release this oath first, and afterward he regrets the core of his oath and they release him. Provided he did not swear upon the mind of the many, for one who swears upon the mind of the many has no release. And 'many' means three, for thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Leviticus 15): זוב דמה ימים רבים — 'days' means two, 'many' means three. And at the beginning of the first chapter of Shevuot: Moses said to Israel, 'Know that it is not upon your mind that I administer the oath to you, but upon my mind and upon the mind of the Omnipresent.' And the Gemara raises the difficulty: let him say to them only 'upon my mind' — why do I need 'upon the mind of the Omnipresent'? So that their oath should have no release." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, explained in his novellae: 'upon the mind of the Omnipresent' refers to the Omnipresent and Knesset Yisrael, which is what is meant by 'he and his court,' and that constitutes the mind of the many and therefore has no release ever; for in the case of Moses they needed this only because he was the emissary of the court of their Father in heaven. And what they said — that one who swears upon the mind of the many has no release — for a matter of mitzvah he does have release, such as a mitzvah that the many need, like a cantor, a scribe, or a teacher for whom there is no equivalent in his place; and this is the opinion of our master R. Tam, of blessed memory, and likewise the opinion of the Rambam, of blessed memory, who wrote in the sixth chapter of the Laws of Oaths: one who swore upon the mind of the many or who vowed upon the mind of the many — they never release it for him, except for a matter of mitzvah. How so? He swore and conditioned his oath upon the mind of the many never to derive benefit from so-and-so forever, and the people of that city needed to learn Torah, or someone to circumcise their children, or to slaughter for them, and none was found but this man alone — he is asked before a sage or three laymen and they release his oath, and he performs these mitzvot for them and takes his wages from those people concerning whom he swore not to derive benefit — end quote. And there is an additional stringency to say: only for a matter similar to that specific mitzvah and not another. But the opinion of Rashi is that one who vowed or swore upon the mind of the many does have release for a matter of mitzvah, for any mitzvah whatsoever; and the reason is that the mind of the many exists only to fulfill mitzvot, not to nullify them. And thus Rashi, of blessed memory, explained in his commentaries on tractate Makkot, chapter אלו הן הגולין, on the passage: such as one who took a vow upon the mind of the many. And this is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, and thus we practice: to release a vow that was taken upon the mind of the many for the sake of any mitzvah."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:55.877058467Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}